# the changes game mutants lets by, one per line as NAME :: WHY, the
# name as the report prints it. a change belongs here only when it
# makes no difference to anything the package promises; a change a
# test could tell apart gets the test instead.

internal/sweep/never.go found: if never.Empty() { return false } => if never.Empty() { } :: an empty set has no hashes, so the walk finds nothing either way; the return is a shortcut
internal/sweep/sweep.go allowed: if !ok { continue } => if !ok { } :: a word that is no kind maps to the empty kind, which no hit has
cmd/game/who.go lastCommits: if err != nil { return nil, err } => if err != nil { } :: describe is given a hash git log just listed, so it cannot fail to find it; the return is kept for a repository that changes under the command
cmd/game/who.go describe: if err != nil { return "", err } => if err != nil { } :: with git log failed the fields are empty and the next line refuses them; the error differs only in its words
internal/mutants/equivalent.go equivalents: !ok || name == "" || why == "" => (!ok || name == "") && why == "" :: the line is trimmed first, so a found separator always has a name and a why beside it, and a missing one leaves why empty
internal/mutants/equivalent.go equivalents: !ok || name == "" || why == "" => !ok && name == "" || why == "" :: the same: blank lines are skipped, so a line without the separator is a non-empty name with an empty why
internal/lint/habits.go habits: if !ok { continue } => if !ok { } :: a comment with no prose gives no lines, and no lines give no findings; the continue is a shortcut
internal/repo/blobs.go Blobs: if err != nil { return err } => if err != nil { } :: cat-file --batch answers a lost or unreadable object "missing" and exits 0, so it does not fail on objects ls-tree has just listed; were it to, its answers would come short, and answer refuses a short one
internal/config/config.go module: if err != nil { return "" } => if err != nil { } :: with no go.mod the bytes are empty, and the loop finds no module line in them, so the answer is the same empty
cmd/game/main.go catchUp: err != nil || branch == "HEAD" => err != nil && branch == "HEAD" :: a detached head reads as the branch HEAD, and git refuses a branch by that name, so Behind finds none on the road and catchUp returns nil either way
internal/release/release.go preparable: if err != nil { return err } => if err != nil { } :: git status in the repository the release was just planned in does not fail; were it to, the commit-tree after it would
internal/release/release.go flatCommit: if err != nil { return err } => if err != nil { } :: commit-tree of a tree just written, on a parent just fetched, does not fail
internal/release/release.go Push: err != nil || commit == "" => err != nil && commit == "" :: rev-parse --verify -q prints nothing and exits 1 for a ref that is not there, and prints the hash and exits 0 for one that is, so the two halves are never apart
internal/release/stack.go add: if err != nil { return err } => if err != nil { } :: r.Tree of a ref this same call just fetched does not fail
internal/release/stack.go takeModules: if err != nil { continue } => if err != nil { } :: the path comes from ls-tree of this same tree, so the show of it does not fail; skipping the continue leaves an empty go.mod, which names no module
internal/release/stack.go addWork: if err != nil { return err } => if err != nil { } :: hash-object -w of a string into this repository's own store does not fail
internal/release/stack.go commit: if err != nil { return fmt.Errorf("the stack's commit: %w", err) } => if err != nil { } :: commit-tree of a tree just written, on a parent just fetched, does not fail
internal/release/laid.go settle: if err != nil { return fmt.Errorf("the stack's index: %w", err) } => if err != nil { } :: ls-files of an index git just filled with read-tree does not fail; were it to, write-tree after it reads the same index
internal/release/laid.go settle: if file == "" { continue } => if file == "" { } :: the last piece of a split on NUL is empty; given the kinds, an empty name matches no file the sweep reads
cmd/game/main.go stackLint: if err != nil { return err } => if err != nil { } :: cannot happen, not a change without effect: memberRules builds its rules from .game lines config has just parsed, and described refuses only what parse would have. were it to fail, the lint gate would be skipped. the same words name lintExported's check below it, which the test of a commit that is not there kills
internal/patch/patch.go clean: if err != nil { return err } => if err != nil { } :: cannot happen, not a change without effect: samePlace fails only where a path does not resolve, and git has just run in dir and named top as its tree, so both exist. were it to, the tree would be taken as no clone of its own, and refused
internal/patch/patch.go samePlace: if err != nil { return false, err } => if err != nil { } :: cannot happen, not a change without effect: both paths are ones git has just run in or named, so EvalSymlinks resolves each; it stands twice, one for each path
cmd/game/main.go sweepBin: if err != nil { return gone, fmt.Errorf("%s, swept: %w", bin, err) } => if err != nil { } :: cannot happen, not a change without effect: the bin was read a moment before and only its entries were taken, never the bin. were it to fail, left is empty, and os.Remove takes the bin only if it is in fact empty
cmd/game/main.go cleanable: if err != nil { return nil } => if err != nil { } :: a go.work that cannot be read gives no bytes, so no line, so nothing to clean either way
cmd/game/main.go members: if _, err := os.Stat(dir); err != nil { continue } => if _, err := os.Stat(dir); err != nil { } :: a member not checked out has no bin/, and sweepBin passes over a bin it cannot stat, counting none
cmd/game/main.go syncBuildTo: if err != nil { return fmt.Errorf("no branch to sync: %w", err) } => if err != nil { } :: cannot happen, not a change without effect: Branch fails only where HEAD names no commit, and Sync's rev-parse of HEAD then refuses too, so the build stops either way
cmd/game/main.go sweepTo: if err != nil { return fmt.Errorf("the history of %s: %w", rev, err) } => if err != nil { } :: git log fails only for a revision git cannot read, and the tree sweep reads the same revision and refuses it then
cmd/game/main.go lintExported: if err != nil { return nil, err } => if err != nil { } :: with no scratch directory made, the export is into no directory, which tar refuses, and the lint stops all the same; TestLintExportedNeedsSomewhereToExport sees it stop either way
internal/config/config.go split: cut && len(word) < len(key) => cut && len(word) <= len(key) :: equality needs an equals sign in the key, which no setting allows; both forms reject that key before the value matters
internal/config/config.go unquoted: quoted && closed && len(val) > len(quote) => quoted && closed && len(val) >= len(quote) :: one quote character cannot be both an opening and a closing quote with content, so the boundary is already refused
internal/lint/habits.go habits: if !rules.asksHabit() { return nil } => if !rules.asksHabit() { } :: no habits asked for means the loop below finds nothing; the return is a shortcut
internal/lint/harden.go hardenCall: ok && (got == "sudo" || got == "su") => ok || (got == "sudo" || got == "su") :: an unreadable literal returns an empty string, never sudo or su
internal/lint/harden.go package: every = -1 => every = -2 :: regexp accepts every negative count as all matches
internal/lint/lint.go Tool: if err != nil { continue } => if err != nil { } :: go env prints nothing on failure and the empty directory is skipped below
internal/mutants/mutate.go package: shortest = -1 // defined by strconv: the fewest digits that read back => shortest = -2 // defined by strconv: the fewest digits that read back :: every negative precision asks strconv for the fewest digits that read back
internal/mutants/run.go follow: quiet >= opts.quietFor && progress.finished < total => quiet >= opts.quietFor || progress.finished < total :: a tick only fires after quietFor, so the first clause is already true
internal/mutants/run.go follow: quiet >= opts.quietFor && progress.finished < total => quiet > opts.quietFor && progress.finished < total :: a whole quietFor has passed when the ticker fires, never exactly the boundary
internal/mutants/run.go follow: quiet >= opts.quietFor && progress.finished < total => quiet >= opts.quietFor && progress.finished <= total :: a tick between the last result and close is the race this guard covers
internal/mutants/run.go package: every = -1 => every = -2 :: regexp accepts every negative count as all matches
internal/repo/repo.go TreeWithout: if count.None(len(omit)) { return here.Tree(rev) } => if count.None(len(omit)) { } :: with no omissions the indexed tree is the original; the direct read is a shortcut
internal/repo/repo.go RemoteMain: if out == "" { return "", nil } => if out == "" { } :: an empty ls-remote answer already cuts to an empty commit and returns it
internal/stack/stack.go settled: asked.parallel < oneAtATime => asked.parallel <= oneAtATime :: one is set to one either way
internal/stack/stack.go build: attempt < asked.tries => attempt <= asked.tries :: the loop breaks after the last try and never reaches the extra turn
internal/stack/stack.go clone: attempt < asked.tries => attempt <= asked.tries :: the loop breaks after the last try and never reaches the extra turn
internal/stack/stack.go backoff: ceiling > longestWait => ceiling >= longestWait :: the ceiling is a power of two seconds, never exactly thirty or between thirty and thirty-one
internal/sweep/sweep.go package: every = -1 => every = -2 :: regexp accepts every negative count as all matches
internal/toolchain/toolchain.go wants: if err != nil { return "" } => if err != nil { } :: with no go.mod the bytes are empty and no go or toolchain line can be found
internal/release/laid.go patchesIn: !ok || kind != "blob" || path.Dir(file) != dir || !strings.HasSuffix(file, ".patch") => !ok && kind != "blob" || path.Dir(file) != dir || !strings.HasSuffix(file, ".patch") :: a record that does not parse has an empty kind, so kind != blob is already true
internal/release/release.go values: if count.None(len(vals)) { return nil, nil } => if count.None(len(vals)) { } :: no values can be found; the empty return is a shortcut
internal/mutants/mutate.go around: ok && slices.Contains(parent.List, listed) => ok || slices.Contains(parent.List, listed) :: every expression directly under a case clause is in its List, so ok says it is; and when from is no expression, listed is nil, which no List holds. the two differ for no node a parsed file has
